# The entry-management alphabet's sequences parked against their findings
# (docs/rune/parity/findings.md):
#
#   v<version>-<seed> <code> <issue>
#
# The line reads as the end of the kept file's name, sorted by version and seed. See
# docs/testing.md, "Fuzzing". Both are F3's browser-view invariant on Rune: the browser's
# Shift anchor outlives an entry that was removed and comes back with it, after Ctrl+Z (0x05)
# and Ctrl+Y (0x04). GTK does the same; the sequences are the seeds'.
# Version 3 adds an Undo with its Redo straight after (F3's undo-redo invariant). Its smoke
# seeds meet two findings of the shared browser and its undo history, both GTK's too: the
# Shift anchor that comes back with a restored entry (lgse/strata#2387, on Rune), and a Redo
# that skips a rename into a new path, does nothing or redoes the operation before it
# (lgse/strata#2435, on both targets). The sequences are the seeds'.
# v3-0000000000000047: a Delete and its Undo bring the browser's anchor back on the entry, as
# the view's is not (F4's round 2).
# v3-0000000000000038: a Shift+click after a folder made by a typed path: GTK selects the entry
# alone, Rune ranges from the folder (F4's round 2).
# v5-0000000000000005: a Delete leaves documents first, then Up: GTK's keyboard goes to the
# header actions (owner.md A1 item 3), the name guard's accepted gap (F4's round 2).
# v5-0000000000000011: a Ctrl+Z that takes an entry out of the root: GTK's keyboard goes to the
# neighbour, Rune's to none (F4's round 2).
# v5-0000000000000002: a click on documents after a Delete: GTK reports notes.txt focused, by
